<section class="page-head">
  <p class="kicker">Part V &middot; Chapter 10 &mdash; with Chapter 16 at its close</p>
  <h1>The Forking Spiral &amp; the Fibonacci Splice</h1>
  <p class="lede muted">Ten cannot be both the return and the next beginning unless it divides. So at ten
    the one thing becomes two: an heir that climbs, and a twin that begins again.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--first illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">The board every being moves across</p>
    <h2 id="t-placed">Zero to One Hundred, Placed</h2>
    <p class="lede">Each decade is one revolution. Angle carries value; radius carries density. Nought
      sits at the centre &mdash; the ground, where the logarithm is undefined and no radius reaches.</p>
    <p>Because the radius grows by log₁₀, the early numbers spread wide and the later ones crowd: the
      denser infinity shows itself as congestion on the page. One, ten and a hundred fall on a single
      radial line &mdash; the same One at three densities, which is the whole claim of the spiral reading
      drawn in a single geometric fact. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p class="muted small">Grading: <span class="sci">&#9670; arithmetic, checkable</span> &middot;
      <span class="spec">&#9670; the framework's reading</span> &middot;
      <span class="myth">&#9670; vision, stated as vision</span>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap">
  <figure id="fig-placed" class="plate">
    <figcaption class="plate-cap"><span class="plate-num">Plate I</span> Zero to One Hundred, Placed
      <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></figcaption>
    <div class="plate-art">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 640 640" role="img" aria-label="A logarithmic spiral of the integers nought to one hundred. Nought is a bright disc at the centre; one, ten and one hundred lie on a single vertical radial line at increasing radius; every other integer is a small circle placed by angle within its decade.">
        <g fill="none" stroke="#24627f" stroke-width="0.5" stroke-dasharray="2 5" opacity="0.8">
          <circle cx="320" cy="320" r="70"/><circle cx="320" cy="320" r="150"/><circle cx="320" cy="320" r="250"/>
        </g>
        <line x1="320" y1="320" x2="320" y2="60" stroke="#76cfee" stroke-width="0.6" stroke-dasharray="4 6" opacity="0.7"/>
        {%- set colours = ["#45d6b4", "#5fc9dd", "#76b8ee", "#8ba2f0", "#a07cf0", "#b983e4", "#cf8ad2", "#e08fc2", "#ef84b6", "#f08aa4"] -%}
        {%- for n in range(1, 101) %}
          {%- set l = n | log10nz -%}
          {%- set rad = 55 + 95 * l -%}
          {%- set frac = l - (l | round(0, "floor")) -%}
          {%- set ang = -1.5708 + 6.28318 * frac -%}
          {%- set cx = (320 + rad * (ang | cos)) | round2 -%}
          {%- set cy = (320 + rad * (ang | sin)) | round2 -%}
          {%- set isTen = (n == 1 or n == 10 or n == 100) -%}
          {%- set isDec = (n % 10 == 0) -%}
        <circle cx="{{ cx }}" cy="{{ cy }}" r="{{ 11 if isTen else (8 if isDec else 5.5) }}"
                fill="{{ '#ffd979' if isTen else 'rgba(3,4,12,0.9)' }}"
                stroke="{{ '#ffd979' if isTen else colours[(n / 10) | round(0, 'floor') % 10] }}"
                stroke-width="{{ 1.6 if isTen else 1 }}"/>
        {%- if isTen or isDec %}
        <text x="{{ cx }}" y="{{ cy + 3 }}" font-family="Cinzel, serif" font-size="{{ 10 if isTen else 8 }}"
              fill="{{ '#03040c' if isTen else '#cdefff' }}" text-anchor="middle">{{ n }}</text>
        {%- endif %}
        {%- endfor %}
        <circle cx="320" cy="320" r="18" fill="#ffd979"/>
        <text x="320" y="325" font-family="Cinzel, serif" font-size="13" fill="#03040c" text-anchor="middle">0</text>
        <text x="320" y="36" font-family="EB Garamond, serif" font-size="12" fill="#ffd979" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">1, 10, 100 — the same One, three densities</text>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <p class="plate-note">Nought is the ground: no radius reaches it, because log is undefined there. The
      crowding of the late eighties and nineties against the open sweep of the single digits is the
      asymmetry of the two infinities, drawn.</p>
  </figure>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">10.1 &mdash; The Rule</p>
    <h2 id="t-splice">At Every Ten, the Path Divides</h2>
    <p class="lede muted">Index the splices n = 1, 2, 3&hellip;, one for each density threshold &mdash;
      10, 100, 1000. Assign the n-th splice a multiplicity equal to the n-th Fibonacci number.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap narrow">
    <div class="frame">
      <p class="formula" style="text-align:center">m<sub>n</sub> = F<sub>n</sub>, &nbsp;
        F₁ = 1, F₂ = 1, F<sub>k</sub> = F<sub>k&minus;1</sub> + F<sub>k&minus;2</sub></p>
      <p>So the first splice spawns one descendant, the second one, the third two, then three, five,
        eight. Within the framework this is not a new postulate. The microtubule lattice &mdash; the
        proposed seat of conscious integration &mdash; was already set at 13 : 8, consecutive Fibonacci
        numbers; the torus chapter set its geometry by the same ratio, giving φ = 2&thinsp;cos(π/5).
        Putting Fibonacci on the splices requires only that the branching of beings obey the law already
        assigned to the lattice they are built from. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <figure id="fig-splice" class="plate">
    <figcaption class="plate-cap"><span class="plate-num">Plate II</span> The Population, Splice by Splice
      <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></figcaption>
    <div class="plate-art">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 760 330" role="img" aria-label="A bar chart of the cumulative population after each splice: one, two, four, seven, twelve, twenty, thirty-three, fifty-four, with each total annotated as a Fibonacci number minus one">
        {%- set totals = [1, 2, 4, 7, 12, 20, 33, 54] -%}
        {%- set spawns = [1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21] -%}
        {%- set fibs = ["F₃−1", "F₄−1", "F₅−1", "F₆−1", "F₇−1", "F₈−1", "F₉−1", "F₁₀−1"] -%}
        {%- for t in totals %}
        <rect x="{{ 60 + loop.index0 * 86 }}" y="{{ 270 - (t * 4) }}" width="52" height="{{ t * 4 }}"
              fill="rgba(69,214,180,0.35)" stroke="#45d6b4" stroke-width="1"/>
        <text x="{{ 86 + loop.index0 * 86 }}" y="{{ 262 - (t * 4) }}" font-family="Cinzel, serif"
              font-size="11" fill="#cdefff" text-anchor="middle">{{ t }}</text>
        <text x="{{ 86 + loop.index0 * 86 }}" y="288" font-family="EB Garamond, serif" font-size="10"
              fill="#a9b6d8" text-anchor="middle">spawns {{ spawns[loop.index0] }}</text>
        <text x="{{ 86 + loop.index0 * 86 }}" y="304" font-family="EB Garamond, serif" font-size="10"
              fill="#ffd979" text-anchor="middle">{{ fibs[loop.index0] }}</text>
        {%- endfor %}
        <text x="380" y="26" font-family="Cinzel, serif" font-size="11" fill="#a9b6d8" letter-spacing="2"
              text-anchor="middle">CUMULATIVE POPULATION AFTER n SPLICES</text>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <p class="plate-note">The ratio of successive totals &mdash; 2, 2, 1.75, 1.714, 1.667, 1.650, 1.636
      &mdash; converges on φ from above. The tree of beings does not double like a colony; it grows like
      a sunflower.</p>
  </figure>

  <div class="wrap narrow">
    <div class="frame illuminated">
      <p class="kicker">10.3</p>
      <h3 id="t-theorem">The Theorem</h3>
      <p class="formula" style="text-align:center">P<sub>n</sub> = &sum;<sub>i=1..n</sub> F<sub>i</sub>
        = F<sub>n+2</sub> &minus; 1</p>
      <p>The cumulative population is not merely near a Fibonacci number; it is exactly one less than
        one. 1 = 2&minus;1, 2 = 3&minus;1, 4 = 5&minus;1, 7 = 8&minus;1, 12 = 13&minus;1, 20 = 21&minus;1.
        The induction is three lines and uses nothing but the defining recurrence.
        <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
      <p class="muted">This is a classical identity, and the site says so plainly. What the framework adds
        is the reading: that the whole tree, summed across every generation, is itself one short of
        completion at every finite stage &mdash; <em>the missing one</em>. The arithmetic is earned; the
        meaning is chosen.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">10.5</p>
    <h2 id="t-666">Where 666 Falls</h2>
    <p class="lede muted">Not the number of dread. A completed accumulation: the sum of every integer
      from 1 to 36, and therefore the thirty-sixth triangular number.</p>
  </div>

  <figure id="fig-666" class="plate">
    <figcaption class="plate-cap"><span class="plate-num">Plate III</span> 666 = T₃₆ = 36 × 37 / 2
      <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></figcaption>
    <div class="plate-art">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 560 330" role="img" aria-label="A triangle of 666 small stones stacked in thirty-six rows, the first row one stone wide and the last thirty-six">
        {%- for row in range(1, 37) %}
          {%- for col in range(0, row) %}
        <rect x="{{ (280 - row * 6.6) + col * 13.2 }}" y="{{ 16 + (row - 1) * 8.2 }}" width="9" height="6"
              rx="1" fill="rgba(69,214,180,0.75)"/>
          {%- endfor %}
        {%- endfor %}
        <g font-family="EB Garamond, serif" font-size="10" fill="#a9b6d8" text-anchor="end">
          <text x="{{ 280 - 1 * 6.6 - 6 }}" y="22">1</text>
          <text x="{{ 280 - 12 * 6.6 - 6 }}" y="{{ 22 + 11 * 8.2 }}">12</text>
          <text x="{{ 280 - 24 * 6.6 - 6 }}" y="{{ 22 + 23 * 8.2 }}">24</text>
          <text x="{{ 280 - 36 * 6.6 - 6 }}" y="{{ 22 + 35 * 8.2 }}">36</text>
        </g>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <p class="plate-note">The Spiral chapter reads the self as the integral &mdash; the running sum of all
      prior moments. A triangular number is exactly that: the total of a complete run. 666 is a number
      that <em>is</em> a finished journey, summed.</p>
  </figure>

  <div class="wrap narrow">
    <div class="frame">
      <h3 id="t-999">666, 333 and the 999 seam</h3>
      <ul class="biblio">
        <li>999 &minus; 666 = 333, and 999 &minus; 333 = 666. The map n &#8596; 999 &minus; n is a
          reflection about 499.5. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></li>
        <li>333, 666 and 999 are 1×, 2× and 3× of 333; 666/999 = 2/3 exactly; the ceiling inverts to the
          ground, 999 &minus; 999 = 0. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></li>
        <li>Only 666 is triangular. 333 and 999 are not. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p><em>Chosen:</em> that this complement-reflection <em>is</em> the framework's Möbius parity
        operation, with 999 as the saturation edge of the third density.
        <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
      <p class="muted"><em>Refused:</em> that &ldquo;6 turns upside-down into 9&rdquo; means anything. That
        is a fact about glyph shapes in a particular typeface, not about numbers &mdash; the same
        borrowed-drama move as the number of the beast, and left out for the same reason.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--creed">
    <p class="kicker">Chapter 16</p>
    <h2 id="t-fixed-points">The Three Fixed Points</h2>
    <p>Euler's identity can be read as a sentence about exactly three values: travelling π through the
      imaginary dimension carries 1 to &minus;1, and the two together resolve to 0. Each is fixed under
      one of the framework's central operations.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Zero &mdash; fixed under scaling.</strong> Multiply it by anything and it is unchanged:
        the one value invariant under every rescaling, every change of density. Exactly the property a
        <em>ground</em> must have.</li>
      <li><strong>One &mdash; fixed under multiplication.</strong> The identity: the value that leaves all
        others unchanged, and so the natural threshold of selfhood.</li>
      <li><strong>Negative one &mdash; fixed under the squared rotation.</strong> i² = &minus;1; it is its
        own reciprocal; squaring returns to +1. It anchors the far side of every seam &mdash; the
        inverted face of the Möbius surface, antimatter to matter, 999 to 0. Where 1 is identity,
        &minus;1 is identity's mirror.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="muted small">Next: <a href="/seams/">Part VI &mdash; the seam, the leaking horizon, and the
      returners</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>
